      "body": "Priority ranking from a full sweep of the open-issue queue on 2026-10-12, against `main` at 1742bfd4. It covers all 93 issues that were open on 2026-10-13 before the eight closures below, including #877, #878 and #880, which were filed during the sweep. It also covers the five issues the sweep filed. Update this issue in place rather than opening a second tracker.\n\nHow the entries were checked:\n- **Re-checked against `main` directly:** the P0 items, the new issues and the closures.\n- **From read-only reviewer checks:** every other entry, each issue checked against `main`.\n\n## P0: wrong or missing identities in files kg-microbe and CultureMech read\n\n- #882: 33 unified rows (3,378 recipe occurrences) have had no identity since #754, because nine merge tombstones no longer resolve to their merge target. PR #881 did not add a tenth: 54e09be6 fixed it before the merge on 2026-10-15.\n- #808: 31 hydrate rows (492 occurrences) still have the anhydrous form or a different compound as their identity. Examples:\n  - the `(NH4)6Mo7O24·4H2O` family maps to ammonium molybdate, CHEBI:91249, instead of CHEBI:86244;\n  - `MnSO4 x n H2O` (209 occurrences) maps to the anhydrous salt.\n  No check catches this.\n- #869: 42 rows publish an anhydrous ChEBI term next to the record's own identity. kg-microbe ranks ChEBI first, so the parent wins.\n- #811, surfacing as #868: wrong label-index answers flow into the unified mapping (e.g. `DL-Tryptophan` → L-tryptophan), including 13 rows that regressed in #863.\n\n## P1\n\n**Wrong identities with a smaller reach**\n- #702: wrong-compound names resolve as unique in the label index, e.g. `Magnesium chloride anhydrous` → the hexahydrate, `carbocerate` → Casamino acids.\n- #878 (closed 2026-10-15): 12 MAPPED labels sat on a different hydrate. It overlaps #702 and #758.\n  - **Fixed:** PR #881 (merged 2026-10-15 as 3751d80f) fixed rows 4-12 and regrounded MgSO4·H2O to `cas:14168-73-1`, with a broadMatch parent.\n  - **Still open:** rows 1-3 (the NaH2PO4 monohydrate spellings) stay with #758.\n  - **Closed by #881's two review rounds:** #892, #893, #895, #897, #898 and #899.\n  - **Filed for later:**\n    - #894: unified rebuild, which waits for culturebotai-claw#604;\n    - #896: Na2so3_X_5_H2o identity;\n    - #900: rejected names with no destination;\n    - #901: 73 closeMatch hydrate anchors;\n    - #902: stale carried review reasons;\n    - #903: untagged replay commit;\n    - #904: visualization counts.\n- #313: `5-didehydro-D-gluconate` resolves to CHEBI:58143; it should be CHEBI:11449.\n- #235: salt names resolve to the free base. Three more records have the same problem and aren't tracked:\n  - `Carnitine_Dl_Hydrochloride`: `cas:461-06-3`, carnitine's CAS, with formula C7H15NO3;\n  - `Sn-Glycerol_3-phosphate_Lithium_Salt`: `cas:17989-41-2`, the free acid's CAS, with formula C3H9O6P;\n  - `Chromium_Iii_Chloride_Hexahydrate`: `cas_rn` 10025-73-7, the anhydrous salt's CAS.\n- #748: \"Cooked meat medium\" resolves to a food term. Blocked by review pins.\n- #751: \"WC Trace Elements Solution\" resolves to the general trace-element-solution class.\n- #758: NaH2PO4 monohydrate spellings sit on the dihydrate record. Blocked by #740.\n- #814: the label index's \"same substance\" verdict covers stereoisomers.\n- #870: six wrong cross-prefix node ids, across 563 occurrences.\n- #753: two invalid CAS identifiers remain (Izalpinin, Methyl-2-Hydroxy-Phenylproprionate). Six more values fail the check digit and aren't tracked:\n  - the identifiers of `D-limonene` (`cas:138-86-7`) and `S-adenosyl_Homocysteine` (`cas:9789-92-0`);\n  - `chemical_properties.cas_rn` in `Kanamycin_Sulfate`, `Methylglyoxal`, `Oxalic_Acid` and `Furaltadone_Hydrochloride`.\n- #877: rebuild the CultureMech membership edges. #877 reports that 3,753 occurrence rows changed identity in CultureMech since the last build. PR #879 does the rebuild, but it is on hold (2026-10-14): CultureMech's aggregator ignores ingredients nested in `solutions[]` (CultureMech#604), so the 41d4d39 edges drop genuine memberships (#891). The CultureMech-side fix is CultureMech#605, which is open.\n- #880: 45 identifiers that CultureMech recipes use have no MIM record, leaving 116 membership edges unlinked (found by the #877 rebuild). Filed on 2026-10-13 after the sweep; its counts were not re-checked here.\n\n**Chemistry**\n- #268, #871: 35 hydrate records carry the anhydrous InChI and SMILES (the 12 in #871 plus 23 more), and these ship in KGX.\n\n**Gates and process**\n- #740, #745: review pins block verified corrections on 40 records, and the \"frozen\" hold constant has been edited by hand twice to let routine work through.\n- #885: the unified-freshness, reviewed-SSSOM and supported-graph checks run on every PR but are not required by the merge queue.\n- #884: the schema describes NARROW_MATCH and BROAD_MATCH the opposite way from `MAPPING_SEMANTICS.md`. This is the root of #696.\n- #883: the \"Latest\" SSSOM release (mim-sssom-2026-09-21) still publishes 37 `exactMatch` rows that `main` has removed.\n- #300: a published `MIM:` id has disappeared again, and nothing checks that the alias map is current.\n- #267: the maintained reground and merge tools have no `just` recipes.\n- #295: 340 records cite research reports that are gitignored.\n- #678: the published maps place unmapped ingredients using made-up embeddings and leave out 533 mapped records.\n\n## P2\n\n- **Decisions only:** #209, #220, #294, #304, #443, #478, #484, #662, #696, #761, #875\n- **Label cleanup:** #232, #587, #655, #656 (retitle it), #669, #816, #873\n- **Withheld rows with no recipe occurrences:** #308, #312, #318, #738, #741, #777\n- **Tooling:** #182, #183, #187, #206, #236, #265, #292, #303, #316, #323, #427, #452, #466, #513, #672, #689, #766, #815, #866\n- **Site and fleet:** #110, #508, #512, #679, #685, #718, #778\n- **Membership freshness:** #886\n\n## Close with their PRs (the fixes exist only on those branches)\n\n- #690 and #691 with PR #688.\n- #765 with PR #764.\n- #680, #681, #682 and #684 with PR #683.\n\n## Close after one step\n\n- **#449:** its remaining items are now #886 and #878.\n- **#492:** there is nothing to do in MIM; move the question to CultureMech.\n- **#467:** the migration it would test can no longer run, so close it as won't-fix.\n- **#459:** narrow it, or close it in favour of the focused issues.\n\n## Closed in this sweep, each with its evidence\n\n#671, #767, #495, #533, #534, #626, #776, #627\n\n## Open PRs\n\n- **#764:** re-cut it on `main`. #817 made the pins stricter without adding a way to supersede one, so #764 is still needed. Carry #765 and fold in #766.\n- **#688:** re-cut it as well; the relabel tool still rebuilds subjects from the label.\n- **#683:** close it and move the #678/#679 fixes into a small new PR. It is 50 commits behind.\n- **#692:** not tied to any open issue, so it was not reviewed.\n- **#879** (for #877): opened on 2026-10-13, after the sweep. Not reviewed here, beyond checking that it leaves tombstone resolution unchanged.\n- **#881** (for #878): merged 2026-10-15 as 3751d80f, after two adversarial review rounds; see the #878 entry above.\n\n## Act on next\n\n1. **#882:** fix the wrong-form synonyms on the nine tombstones, set `representative:`, rebuild, and add a gate.\n2. **#869 and #808:** add rejection-ledger rows the way #863 did, move each label to the correct record, and add the hydrate → anhydrous check #808 asks for.\n3. **Re-cut #764.** That fixes #740 and #745 and unblocks #748, #758 and #741.\n\nOther signals:\n- 25 issues were more than 60 days old on 2026-10-12; the oldest, #110, was 100 days.\n- `NEXT_TASKS.md` covers 10 of the open issues.\n\n\n\n\n",
