      "body": "Found during the #320 triage.\n\nA live record has `preferred_term: \"D\"` — a single character — mapped to `CHEBI:29958 L-aspartic acid residue`, and carried `cas_rn: 138-86-3`, which is **limonene**.\n\nThree fields, three different substances implied:\n\n| field | value | denotes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `preferred_term` | `D` | nothing determinate |\n| `ontology_id` | `CHEBI:29958` | L-aspartic acid *residue* (a polymer residue, not a free compound) |\n| `cas_rn` | `138-86-3` | limonene |\n\nThe CAS was withdrawn in #345 as demonstrably false, but that is the smallest problem here. Two structural issues remain:\n\n1. **The label is almost certainly a parsing artifact.** A single `D` suggests a truncated or mis-split field upstream — a one-letter amino-acid code, a column shift, or a stray token from a recipe table. Which of those it is determines whether the record should be relabelled, merged, or deleted.\n2. **`CHEBI:29958` is a residue term, not a substance term.** Residue classes describe a unit *within* a polymer and should generally not be the mapping target for a growth-medium ingredient at all, independent of what the label turns out to mean. Worth checking whether other records are also grounded to `...residue` terms.\n\nResolving this needs the upstream source row (which recipe, which column), not chemistry — so it is filed rather than guessed at. If the source cannot be recovered, the honest outcome is to tombstone the record rather than leave three mutually contradictory fields published.",
      "closed_by": {
